<!-- CHAPTER 1: INTRODUCTION AND BACKGROUND -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="page-break"></div>
<h1>CHAPTER 1: INTRODUCTION AND BACKGROUND</h1>

<h2>1.1 Introduction</h2>
<p>Educational institutions, particularly primary schools in developing countries, face significant challenges in managing student fee records and financial transactions. The traditional approach of manual fee collection, paper-based receipting, and physical ledger maintenance is increasingly inadequate in the face of growing student populations and the demand for transparency and efficiency. FeeFlow is a comprehensive web-based school fee management system developed to address these challenges by providing a digital platform for fee administration, online payments, appointment scheduling, and financial analytics.</p>
<p>The system was developed as a final year project for the Master of Science in Information Technology program at ISBAT University. It builds upon established principles of web application development, database management, and user experience design to deliver a practical solution for real-world school fee management needs.</p>

<h2>1.2 Background of the Study</h2>
<p>School fee management is a critical administrative function in any educational institution. The process involves setting fee structures, collecting payments from students and parents, issuing receipts, tracking outstanding balances, managing payment plans, and generating financial reports. In many Ugandan schools, these processes are still carried out manually using ledgers, carbon-copy receipt books, and Excel spreadsheets.</p>
<p>The manual approach presents several inherent problems. Fee records can be lost, damaged, or altered. Calculation errors in totaling fees or computing balances are common. The absence of real-time visibility into fee collection status makes it difficult for school administrators to monitor financial health. Students and parents must physically visit the school to make payments, check balances, or discuss fee-related issues, often resulting in long queues and wasted time.</p>
<p>The rapid advancement of information and communication technology (ICT) in Uganda, particularly the widespread adoption of mobile money services such as MTN Mobile Money and MTN Money, presents an opportunity to revolutionize school fee management. By integrating digital payment channels with a centralized fee management platform, schools can offer parents and students the convenience of paying fees from anywhere, at any time, while maintaining accurate and transparent financial records.</p>
<p>Several fee management solutions exist in the market, including commercial products like SchoolPay, Edsys, and Fedena. However, these solutions often come with high licensing costs, complex implementation requirements, and limited customization options for local school needs. FeeFlow was designed as a lightweight, affordable, and customizable alternative that specifically addresses the requirements of Ugandan primary schools.</p>

<h2>1.3 Problem Statement</h2>
<p>Schools in Uganda face significant challenges in managing student fee collections effectively. The specific problems identified include:</p>
<ol>
<li><b>Inefficient Payment Processing:</b> Parents must physically visit the school to pay fees, leading to long queues, wasted time, and inconvenience. The lack of digital payment options limits accessibility and flexibility.</li>
<li><b>Poor Record Keeping:</b> Manual fee records are prone to errors, loss, and damage. Tracking individual student payment histories and outstanding balances is difficult and time-consuming.</li>
<li><b>Limited Transparency:</b> Students and parents cannot easily access fee status information. There is no self-service portal for checking balances, viewing payment history, or downloading receipts.</li>
<li><b>Ineffective Communication:</b> Schools struggle to send timely reminders about fee deadlines and overdue payments. Communication is often ad-hoc and relies on physical notices or word of mouth.</li>
<li><b>Appointment and Queue Management:</b> Students visiting the finance office must wait in unpredictable queues. There is no system for booking appointments or tracking queue progress.</li>
<li><b>Lack of Financial Analytics:</b> School administrators lack tools for analyzing fee collection trends, identifying at-risk students, and generating comprehensive financial reports.</li>
</ol>

<h2>1.4 Objectives of the Study</h2>
<p><b>Main Objective:</b> To design, develop, and implement a comprehensive web-based school fee management system that digitizes fee collection, payment tracking, and financial reporting for primary schools.</p>
<p><b>Specific Objectives:</b></p>
<ol>
<li>To design a secure, scalable database schema for storing student information, fee structures, payment records, and appointment data.</li>
<li>To develop an administrative dashboard for managing students, monitoring payments, generating reports, and sending reminders.</li>
<li>To create a student self-service portal for viewing fee status, making online payments, booking appointments, and accessing payment history.</li>
<li>To integrate multiple digital payment channels including Mobile Money, Credit/Debit Cards, Bank Transfer, USSD, and QR Code payments.</li>
<li>To implement a real-time appointment booking and queue monitoring system for the finance office.</li>
<li>To build comprehensive analytics and reporting capabilities for financial decision-making.</li>
<li>To test and validate the system using realistic seed data across multiple class levels and fee types.</li>
</ol>

<h2>1.5 Research Questions</h2>
<ol>
<li>What are the key challenges faced by schools in managing student fee collections?</li>
<li>How can a web-based fee management system improve the efficiency of fee collection and tracking?</li>
<li>What are the essential functional requirements for a comprehensive school fee management system?</li>
<li>How can multiple digital payment channels be effectively integrated into a unified fee management platform?</li>
<li>What database architecture best supports the transactional and real-time data needs of a fee management system?</li>
</ol>

<h2>1.6 Scope of the Study</h2>
<p>The scope of this project encompasses the design, development, and testing of a fee management system for primary schools in Uganda. The system covers the full lifecycle of fee administration including: student registration and management, fee structure definition, payment processing through multiple channels, receipt generation, appointment booking, queue management, payment plan management, reminder notifications, and financial analytics.</p>
<p>The system is designed for two categories of users: administrators (school staff) and students. The administrative interface provides comprehensive management capabilities while the student portal focuses on self-service access to fee information and payment functionality. The system uses MySQL for transactional data storage and Firebase Firestore for real-time features including notifications and queue updates.</p>
<p>Testing was conducted using seed data for 16 students spanning 8 class levels from Nursery to Primary 7, with various fee types including Tuition, Development, Sports, Library, and School Meals fees, and multiple payment methods.</p>

<h2>1.7 Significance of the Study</h2>
<p>The FeeFlow system contributes to the digital transformation of school financial management in Uganda. The significance of this study includes:</p>
<ol>
<li><b>For Schools:</b> Improved efficiency in fee collection, reduced administrative workload, accurate financial record keeping, and enhanced transparency.</li>
<li><b>For Students and Parents:</b> Convenience of online payments, easy access to fee information, ability to book appointments, and reduced time spent in queues.</li>
<li><b>For Administrators:</b> Real-time visibility into fee collection status, comprehensive analytics for informed decision-making, and tools for proactive communication with stakeholders.</li>
<li><b>For the Academic Community:</b> A practical demonstration of web application development principles, database design, and payment system integration that can serve as a reference for similar projects.</li>
</ol>

<h2>1.8 Limitations of the Study</h2>
<p>The project has the following limitations:</p>
<ol>
<li>The system was developed as a prototype and has not been deployed in a live school environment.</li>
<li>Payment processing is simulated and does not integrate with actual payment gateway APIs.</li>
<li>The system was tested with synthetic seed data rather than real school data.</li>
<li>Firebase integration is limited to Firestore database features; Firebase Authentication and Cloud Functions were not fully implemented.</li>
<li>The system focuses on primary school fee management and may require modifications for secondary or tertiary institution use.</li>
</ol>

<h2>1.9 Organization of the Report</h2>
<p>This report is organized into eight chapters. Chapter 1 provides the introduction and background. Chapter 2 presents the system functional specification including requirements and user interface design. Chapter 3 discusses system performance requirements. Chapter 4 describes the system design overview and architecture. Chapter 5 details the data structure specifications and database design. Chapter 6 presents the module design specifications. Chapter 7 covers system verification and testing. Chapter 8 provides conclusions and recommendations. The report concludes with a bibliography and appendices containing program listings and the user manual.</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!-- CHAPTER 2: SYSTEM FUNCTIONAL SPECIFICATION -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="page-break"></div>
<h1>CHAPTER 2: SYSTEM FUNCTIONAL SPECIFICATION</h1>

<h2>2.1 Introduction</h2>
<p>This chapter presents the functional and non-functional requirements of the FeeFlow system, use case diagrams, user interface design, input/output specifications, and error handling mechanisms. The requirements were gathered through analysis of existing school fee management processes and identification of pain points that the system aims to address.</p>

<h2>2.2 Functional Requirements</h2>

<h3>2.2.1 Admin Module Functions</h3>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:10%;">ID</th><th style="width:40%;">Function</th><th style="width:50%;">Description</th></tr>
<tr><td>ADM-01</td><td>Admin Authentication</td><td>Secure login and logout using email/username and password credentials with session management.</td></tr>
<tr><td>ADM-02</td><td>Student Management</td><td>View, search, filter, and delete student records. Display comprehensive student information including personal details, fee status, and payment progress.</td></tr>
<tr><td>ADM-03</td><td>Payment Dashboard</td><td>View real-time financial summary showing total collections, outstanding balances, and recovery rate with drill-down capabilities.</td></tr>
<tr><td>ADM-04</td><td>Online Payment Monitoring</td><td>Track all online payment transactions with filtering by date, status, and payment method. Verify pending payments.</td></tr>
<tr><td>ADM-05</td><td>Payment Analytics</td><td>Comprehensive analytics including collection breakdown by fee type, payment method distribution, and top outstanding students.</td></tr>
<tr><td>ADM-06</td><td>Send Reminders</td><td>Send batch payment reminders to students with overdue fees. Select overdue period and view overdue student list.</td></tr>
<tr><td>ADM-07</td><td>Firebase Student View</td><td>View student data synchronized from MySQL to Firebase Firestore with real-time capabilities.</td></tr>
<tr><td>ADM-08</td><td>CSV Data Export</td><td>Export filtered student list data to CSV format for offline analysis and record keeping.</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>2.2.2 Student Module Functions</h3>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:10%;">ID</th><th style="width:40%;">Function</th><th style="width:50%;">Description</th></tr>
<tr><td>STU-01</td><td>Student Dashboard</td><td>Personalized dashboard showing welcome message, fee summary (total paid, pending, clearance status), fee breakdown, payment history table, and real-time notifications.</td></tr>
<tr><td>STU-02</td><td>Online Payment Gateway</td><td>Secure payment interface with payment summary, multiple payment methods, and form-based payment submission.</td></tr>
<tr><td>STU-03</td><td>Payment History</td><td>Complete transaction history with filtering by status and date range. View receipts for paid transactions.</td></tr>
<tr><td>STU-04</td><td>Skip the Line – Pay Online</td><td>Landing page promoting digital payment benefits with direct links to payment gateway and appointment booking.</td></tr>
<tr><td>STU-05</td><td>Payment Receipt</td><td>Detailed payment receipt with student information, amount paid, payment method, transaction reference, and print functionality.</td></tr>
<tr><td>STU-06</td><td>Appointment Booking</td><td>Book appointments with the finance office by selecting date, time slot, and purpose. View upcoming appointments.</td></tr>
<tr><td>STU-07</td><td>Payment Plans</td><td>View available installment plans (Standard 3-month, Extended 6-month, Quick Pay 2-month) and plan history.</td></tr>
<tr><td>STU-08</td><td>Dashboard Alerts</td><td>View overdue payments and upcoming deadlines with days overdue/remaining and direct payment links.</td></tr>
<tr><td>STU-09</td><td>Queue Monitor</td><td>Live queue display showing currently serving token, estimated wait time, and full queue list.</td></tr>
<tr><td>STU-10</td><td>Financial Aid</td><td>Information page about available financial assistance programs.</td></tr>
<tr><td>STU-11</td><td>Documents</td><td>Upload and manage supporting documents for financial aid and fee-related requests.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>2.3 Non-Functional Requirements</h2>

<h3>2.3.1 Security Requirements</h3>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:10%;">ID</th><th style="width:30%;">Requirement</th><th style="width:60%;">Description</th></tr>
<tr><td>SEC-01</td><td>Password Hashing</td><td>All user passwords must be hashed using bcrypt (PHP password_hash with PASSWORD_DEFAULT) before storage.</td></tr>
<tr><td>SEC-02</td><td>Authentication Control</td><td>Admin pages must verify user authentication before allowing access. Unauthenticated requests must be redirected to the login page.</td></tr>
<tr><td>SEC-03</td><td>SQL Injection Prevention</td><td>Database queries must use mysqli_real_escape_string for user-supplied values.</td></tr>
<tr><td>SEC-04</td><td>Session Management</td><td>PHP sessions must be used for maintaining user login state with session_start() and session_destroy() for logout.</td></tr>
<tr><td>SEC-05</td><td>XSS Prevention</td><td>All user-supplied data must be sanitized using htmlspecialchars() before display in HTML output.</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>2.3.2 Usability Requirements</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>ID</th><th>Requirement</th><th>Description</th></tr>
<tr><td>USR-01</td><td>Responsive Design</td><td>The user interface must be responsive and functional across desktop, tablet, and mobile devices.</td></tr>
<tr><td>USR-02</td><td>Intuitive Navigation</td><td>The system must provide clear navigation paths with organized menu structures and quick action links.</td></tr>
<tr><td>USR-03</td><td>Real-Time Feedback</td><td>User actions must provide immediate visual feedback including loading indicators, success/error messages, and toast notifications.</td></tr>
<tr><td>USR-04</td><td>Consistent Styling</td><td>All pages must follow a consistent visual design using a unified CSS stylesheet.</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>2.3.3 Performance Requirements</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>ID</th><th>Requirement</th><th>Target</th></tr>
<tr><td>PER-01</td><td>Page Load Time</td><td>All pages must load within 3 seconds on standard broadband connection.</td></tr>
<tr><td>PER-02</td><td>Query Response</td><td>Database queries must return results within 2 seconds for datasets up to 10,000 records.</td></tr>
<tr><td>PER-03</td><td>Concurrent Users</td><td>System must support at least 50 concurrent users without significant performance degradation.</td></tr>
<tr><td>PER-04</td><td>Real-Time Updates</td><td>Firestore-based real-time features must update within 5 seconds of data changes.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>2.4 Use Case Diagrams</h2>

<h3>2.4.1 Admin Use Cases</h3>
<p>The admin user interacts with the system through the following primary use cases:</p>
<ol>
<li><b>Authenticate:</b> Admin logs in with email/username and password credentials to access the admin dashboard.</li>
<li><b>Manage Students:</b> Admin views the student registry, searches and filters students, views detailed profiles, and deletes student records.</li>
<li><b>Monitor Payments:</b> Admin accesses the payment dashboard to view collection statistics, recent transactions, and fee-type breakdowns.</li>
<li><b>Manage Online Payments:</b> Admin monitors incoming online payment transactions, filters by status, and verifies pending payments.</li>
<li><b>View Analytics:</b> Admin accesses advanced analytics including collection rates, payment method distribution, and outstanding balance reports.</li>
<li><b>Send Reminders:</b> Admin sends batch payment reminders to students with overdue fees based on selected overdue period.</li>
<li><b>Export Data:</b> Admin exports student and payment data to CSV format for external analysis.</li>
</ol>

<h3>2.4.2 Student Use Cases</h3>
<p>The student user interacts with the system through the following primary use cases:</p>
<ol>
<li><b>View Dashboard:</b> Student logs in and views personalized dashboard with fee summary, payment history, and notifications.</li>
<li><b>Make Payment:</b> Student accesses payment gateway, selects pending fees, chooses payment method, and submits payment.</li>
<li><b>View Payment History:</b> Student views complete transaction records with filtering capabilities.</li>
<li><b>Book Appointment:</b> Student selects date and time slot, specifies purpose, and books an appointment with the finance office.</li>
<li><b>View Payment Plans:</b> Student explores available installment plans and views plan history.</li>
<li><b>View Queue Status:</b> Student monitors the live queue to see current serving token and estimated wait time.</li>
<li><b>View Receipts:</b> Student views and prints payment receipts for completed transactions.</li>
<li><b>View Reminders:</b> Student checks overdue payments and upcoming deadlines with direct payment links.</li>
</ol>

<h2>2.5 User Interface Design</h2>

<h3>2.5.1 Design Principles</h3>
<p>The FeeFlow user interface follows these design principles:</p>
<ol>
<li><b>Clarity:</b> Information is presented in a clear, organized manner using visual hierarchy, consistent typography, and meaningful iconography.</li>
<li><b>Efficiency:</b> Common tasks such as making payments, viewing balances, and booking appointments can be completed in minimal steps through prominent action buttons and quick access menus.</li>
<li><b>Feedback:</b> All user actions trigger appropriate feedback including success messages, error alerts, loading states, and confirmation dialogs.</li>
<li><b>Consistency:</b> Colors, fonts, spacing, button styles, and layout patterns are consistent throughout the application for predictable user experience.</li>
</ol>

<h3>2.5.2 Color Scheme and Typography</h3>
<p>The system uses a modern, professional color scheme:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Primary Color:</b> #4f46e5 (Indigo) – Used for primary buttons, links, headers, and key UI elements.</li>
<li><b>Success Color:</b> #10b981 (Emerald) – Used for paid status indicators, success messages, and positive metrics.</li>
<li><b>Danger Color:</b> #ef4444 (Red) – Used for overdue payments, error messages, and critical alerts.</li>
<li><b>Warning Color:</b> #f59e0b (Amber) – Used for pending status, upcoming deadlines, and moderate alerts.</li>
<li><b>Background:</b> #f8fafc (Light gray) – Clean, neutral background for content areas.</li>
<li><b>Text:</b> #1e293b (Dark slate) – Primary text color with #64748b for muted/secondary text.</li>
</ul>
<p>The typography uses the Inter font family with fallback to system fonts, ensuring readability across devices.</p>

<h3>2.5.3 Admin Dashboard Layout</h3>
<p>The admin dashboard features a top navigation bar with the system logo and user menu, a main content area with summary statistic cards, data tables, and action buttons. The layout is designed for efficient monitoring and management of school fee operations.</p>

<h3>2.5.4 Student Dashboard Layout</h3>
<p>The student dashboard features a welcome section with student information, a quick access menu with icon-based navigation cards, fee summary statistics, fee breakdown sidebar, and payment history table. A profile modal provides detailed student information.</p>

<h2>2.6 Input/Output Specifications</h2>

<h3>2.6.1 Payment Input Form</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Field</th><th>Type</th><th>Validation</th><th>Example</th></tr>
<tr><td>Student ID</td><td>Hidden (auto-filled)</td><td>Must match existing student_id</td><td>S101</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fee Amount</td><td>Auto-calculated</td><td>Must be positive number</td><td>800.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>Card Number</td><td>Text input</td><td>16-digit numeric</td><td>1234 5678 9012 3456</td></tr>
<tr><td>Expiry Date</td><td>Text input</td><td>MM/YY format, future date</td><td>12/27</td></tr>
<tr><td>CVV</td><td>Text input</td><td>3-4 digit numeric</td><td>123</td></tr>
<tr><td>Phone Number</td><td>Tel input</td><td>Valid phone format</td><td>+256 7xx xxx xxx</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mobile Provider</td><td>Select dropdown</td><td>Must select one option</td><td>MTN Mobile Money</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>2.6.2 Appointment Booking Input Form</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Field</th><th>Type</th><th>Validation</th><th>Example</th></tr>
<tr><td>Student ID</td><td>Hidden (auto-filled)</td><td>Must match existing student_id</td><td>S101</td></tr>
<tr><td>Appointment Date</td><td>Date picker</td><td>Must be between tomorrow and 30 days from now</td><td>2026-06-15</td></tr>
<tr><td>Time Slot</td><td>Selectable grid</td><td>Must be available time slot</td><td>09:00:00</td></tr>
<tr><td>Purpose</td><td>Select dropdown</td><td>Must select one option</td><td>Pay School Fees</td></tr>
<tr><td>Additional Notes</td><td>Textarea</td><td>Optional, max 500 chars</td><td>Will bring payment receipts</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>2.6.3 Output: Payment Receipt</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Field</th><th>Description</th></tr>
<tr><td>Receipt Number</td><td>Auto-generated unique identifier (RCP-000001)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Date & Time</td><td>Timestamp of payment processing</td></tr>
<tr><td>Student Name</td><td>Full name from student record</td></tr>
<tr><td>Student ID</td><td>Unique student identifier</td></tr>
<tr><td>Class</td><td>Current class/grade level</td></tr>
<tr><td>Payment Method</td><td>Selected payment channel</td></tr>
<tr><td>Amount Paid</td><td>Monetary amount in UGX and USD</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fees Covered</td><td>List of fee items cleared</td></tr>
<tr><td>Transaction Reference</td><td>Bank reference number (if applicable)</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>2.7 Error Handling</h2>
<p>The system implements comprehensive error handling throughout all modules:</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Scenario</th><th>Error Handling Strategy</th></tr>
<tr><td>Database Connection Failure</td><td>Connection check with die() and mysqli_connect_error() for debugging. The db.php script terminates with error message.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Student Not Found</td><td>Dashboard page checks for null student result and displays user-friendly error message with navigation link home.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Invalid Payment Submission</td><td>Server-side validation checks for required fields and valid data formats before processing payment.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Appointment Time Slot Full</td><td>Server checks count of existing appointments for the requested time slot. If >=5, displays error message prompting user to select another time.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Insert Failure</td><td>SQL insert operations check mysqli_query return value and display appropriate success/error messages.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Firebase Data Error</td><td>JavaScript try/catch blocks wrap all Firestore operations with console.error logging and user-friendly fallback messages.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Authentication Failure</td><td>Admin pages redirect unauthorized users to login page. Invalid credentials display error message on login form.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Delete Operation</td><td>Student deletion shows confirmation modal before proceeding. Cascade deletes associated fee, appointment, and queue records.</td></tr>
</table>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!-- CHAPTER 3: SYSTEM PERFORMANCE REQUIREMENTS -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="page-break"></div>
<h1>CHAPTER 3: SYSTEM PERFORMANCE REQUIREMENTS</h1>

<h2>3.1 Introduction</h2>
<p>This chapter defines the performance requirements for the FeeFlow system, including efficiency metrics, reliability measures, security requirements, scalability considerations, and error recovery mechanisms. These requirements ensure that the system delivers acceptable performance under expected usage conditions.</p>

<h2>3.2 Performance Metrics</h2>

<h3>3.2.1 Response Time Requirements</h3>
<p>The system must meet the following response time benchmarks under normal operating conditions (single user, local network):</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Operation</th><th>Target Response Time</th><th>Measurement Method</th></tr>
<tr><td>Page Load (Dashboard)</td><td>&lt; 2 seconds</td><td>Browser dev tools network tab</td></tr>
<tr><td>Student Data Query</td><td>&lt; 1 second</td><td>PHP microtime() before/after query</td></tr>
<tr><td>Payment Submission</td><td>&lt; 2 seconds</td><td>Server-side execution time</td></tr>
<tr><td>Appointment Booking</td><td>&lt; 1.5 seconds</td><td>Form submit to confirmation display</td></tr>
<tr><td>Real-time Notification</td><td>&lt; 5 seconds</td><td>Firestore onSnapshot callback latency</td></tr>
<tr><td>Data Export (CSV)</td><td>&lt; 3 seconds</td><td>File generation and download time</td></tr>
<tr><td>Reminder Batch Send</td><td>&lt; 5 seconds</td><td>SQL update execution + response</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>3.2.2 Throughput Requirements</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Metric</th><th>Target</th></tr>
<tr><td>Maximum concurrent student users</td><td>50</td></tr>
<tr><td>Maximum concurrent admin users</td><td>10</td></tr>
<tr><td>Daily payment transactions processed</td><td>500</td></tr>
<tr><td>Daily appointment bookings</td><td>100</td></tr>
<tr><td>Database query throughput</td><td>100 queries/second</td></tr>
<tr><td>Firestore real-time listeners</td><td>60 simultaneous</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>3.3 Reliability and Availability</h2>

<h3>3.3.1 System Availability</h3>
<p>The FeeFlow system targets 99.5% availability during school operating hours (Monday-Friday, 8:00 AM to 5:00 PM). The system operates on a local XAMPP server environment which provides reliable runtime for development and testing purposes. Key reliability measures include:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Database Persistence:</b> MySQL provides ACID-compliant transaction support ensuring data integrity during payment processing.</li>
<li><b>Session Persistence:</b> PHP session management maintains user login state across page requests.</li>
<li><b>Data Redundancy:</b> Dual database architecture (MySQL + Firebase Firestore) provides backup for critical student records.</li>
<li><b>Graceful Degradation:</b> If Firebase is unavailable, the system continues to function using MySQL-only features.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3.3.2 Backup and Recovery</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Component</th><th>Backup Strategy</th><th>Recovery Procedure</th></tr>
<tr><td>MySQL Database</td><td>Export SQL dump via phpMyAdmin or mysqldump command</td><td>Import SQL dump to restore all tables</td></tr>
<tr><td>Firebase Firestore</td><td>Automatic cloud backup via Firebase console</td><td>Restore from Firebase console or re-sync data</td></tr>
<tr><td>CSS/JS Assets</td><td>Git version control and local file backup</td><td>Replace files from backup or git checkout</td></tr>
<tr><td>PHP Scripts</td><td>Git version control</td><td>git clone or git pull to restore</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>3.4 Security Requirements</h2>

<h3>3.4.1 Authentication Security</h3>
<p>The admin authentication system implements the following security measures:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>bcrypt Password Hashing:</b> All passwords are stored using PHP's password_hash() function with the PASSWORD_DEFAULT algorithm (currently bcrypt), which includes automatic salting and adaptive hashing.</li>
<li><b>Session-Based Authentication:</b> Admin authentication is managed through PHP sessions. The auth.php script checks $_SESSION['admin_logged_in'] before any admin page content is rendered.</li>
<li><b>Login Attempt Management:</b> The login form displays error messages for invalid credentials without revealing whether the username or password was incorrect.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3.4.2 Data Security</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>SQL Injection Prevention:</b> All user inputs used in database queries are sanitized using mysqli_real_escape_string() before query execution.</li>
<li><b>Cross-Site Scripting Prevention:</b> All dynamic data displayed in HTML is escaped using htmlspecialchars() to prevent XSS attacks.</li>
<li><b>Input Validation:</b> Server-side validation of form inputs before processing payments, appointments, and other sensitive operations.</li>
<li><b>Firestore Security:</b> Firebase security rules should be configured to restrict read/write access to authenticated users only.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3.4.3 Payment Data Security</h3>
<p>While the current implementation uses simulated payment processing, the system is designed with security considerations for future real payment gateway integration:</p>
<ul>
<li>Sensitive payment fields (card numbers, CVV) should be processed through PCI-compliant third-party payment gateways.</li>
<li>Payment form data should be transmitted over HTTPS in production deployment.</li>
<li>No raw payment credentials are stored in the database; only payment status and reference numbers are retained.</li>
</ul>

<h2>3.5 Scalability Considerations</h2>
<p>The FeeFlow system architecture supports scalability through the following design decisions:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Modular Code Organization:</b> PHP files are organized by module (admin/, student/) with shared components (db.php, header.php, footer.php) for easy maintenance and extension.</li>
<li><b>Database Indexing:</b> The MySQL schema uses primary keys and foreign key relationships for efficient querying as data volume grows.</li>
<li><b>Query Optimization:</b> SQL queries use aggregate functions (SUM, COUNT, GROUP BY) with JOINs optimized for the expected data relationships.</li>
<li><b>Firebase Real-Time Scaling:</b> Firestore automatically scales to handle increasing numbers of concurrent listeners and document reads.</li>
<li><b>Limit Clauses:</b> Data queries use LIMIT clauses (e.g., LIMIT 30, LIMIT 50) to prevent excessive data retrieval as the database grows.</li>
</ul>
<p>For production deployment, the following scalability enhancements are recommended:</p>
<ol>
<li>Migrate from MySQL to a cloud database service (e.g., Amazon RDS, Google Cloud SQL).</li>
<li>Implement server-side caching using Redis or Memcached for frequently accessed data.</li>
<li>Deploy on cloud infrastructure with auto-scaling capabilities.</li>
<li>Use a CDN for static asset delivery to improve global response times.</li>
</ol>

<h2>3.6 Error Recovery and Fault Tolerance</h2>

<h3>3.6.1 Error Recovery Procedures</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Failure Scenario</th><th>Detection Method</th><th>Recovery Procedure</th></tr>
<tr><td>MySQL connection failure</td><td>mysqli_connect() returns false</td><td>Display maintenance page, check MySQL service status, restart XAMPP</td></tr>
<tr><td>Firebase SDK load failure</td><td>typeof firebase === 'undefined' check</td><td>Display console warning, fall back to MySQL-only features</td></tr>
<tr><td>PHP script error</td><td>PHP error reporting or exception handling</td><td>Check PHP error logs, fix syntax/runtime errors, reload page</td></tr>
<tr><td>Data integrity issue</td><td>SQL query returns unexpected results</td><td>Restore from database backup, re-run schema migration if needed</td></tr>
<tr><td>Session timeout</td><td>Auth check returns false</td><td>Redirect to login page, user re-authenticates</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>3.6.2 Fault Tolerance Features</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Graceful Exception Handling:</b> Try/catch blocks in JavaScript for Firebase operations prevent script crashes and provide user-friendly fallbacks.</li>
<li><b>Transaction Integrity:</b> MySQL's ACID compliance ensures that payment records are either fully committed or fully rolled back in case of failure.</li>
<li><b>Session Redundancy:</b> PHP sessions maintain user state across page loads, allowing recovery from interrupted requests.</li>
<li><b>Data Validation:</b> Both client-side (HTML5 validation) and server-side (PHP validation) checks prevent invalid data from entering the system.</li>
</ul>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!-- CHAPTER 4: SYSTEM DESIGN OVERVIEW -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="page-break"></div>
<h1>CHAPTER 4: SYSTEM DESIGN OVERVIEW</h1>

<h2>4.1 Introduction</h2>
<p>This chapter presents the overall design of the FeeFlow system including system architecture, data flow diagrams, technology stack justification, equipment configuration, and network architecture. The design follows established software engineering principles to create a maintainable, scalable, and secure fee management solution.</p>

<h2>4.2 System Architecture</h2>
<p>The FeeFlow system adopts a three-tier client-server architecture consisting of the presentation tier (frontend), application tier (backend), and data tier (database). This architecture separates concerns and allows independent development and maintenance of each layer.</p>

<h3>4.2.1 Presentation Tier (Frontend)</h3>
<p>The presentation layer consists of HTML5 pages, CSS3 stylesheets, and JavaScript scripts that run in the user's web browser. This layer is responsible for:</p>
<ul>
<li>Rendering the user interface for both admin and student modules</li>
<li>Handling user interactions through forms, buttons, and navigation elements</li>
<li>Client-side form validation using HTML5 validation attributes</li>
<li>Real-time UI updates through Firebase Firestore listeners</li>
<li>Sending AJAX requests to the server when needed</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Technologies Used:</b> HTML5, CSS3, JavaScript (vanilla), Firebase JavaScript SDK v8.10.1</p>

<h3>4.2.2 Application Tier (Backend)</h3>
<p>The application layer processes requests from the frontend, executes business logic, and interacts with the data tier. This layer is responsible for:</p>
<ul>
<li>User authentication and session management</li>
<li>Processing payment submissions and updating fee records</li>
<li>Managing appointment bookings with conflict checking</li>
<li>Generating analytics data and reports</li>
<li>Sending batch payment reminders</li>
<li>Handling data export operations</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Technologies Used:</b> PHP 8.x, Apache web server (via XAMPP)</p>

<h3>4.2.3 Data Tier (Database)</h3>
<p>The data tier stores and manages all system data. FeeFlow uses a dual database architecture:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>MySQL Database:</b> Primary data store for transactional data including students, fees, payments, appointments, queue, staff, and payment deadlines tables. Provides ACID-compliant transaction support for financial data integrity.</li>
<li><b>Firebase Firestore:</b> Real-time NoSQL database for notifications, messages, and student profiles that require real-time synchronization across clients.</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Technologies Used:</b> MySQL 8.x (via XAMPP phpMyAdmin), Firebase Firestore (cloud-hosted)</p>

<h2>4.3 Data Flow Diagrams</h2>

<h3>4.3.1 Context Level Diagram (Level 0)</h3>
<p>At the context level, the FeeFlow system interacts with two external entities:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Administrator:</b> Manages students, monitors payments, sends reminders, views analytics, and exports data.</li>
<li><b>Student:</b> Views dashboard, makes payments, books appointments, views queue, checks reminders, and views receipts.</li>
</ul>

<h3>4.3.2 Level 1 Data Flow Diagram – Admin Functions</h3>
<p><b>Process 1: Student Management</b></p>
<ul>
<li>Input: Filter criteria (search text, class, gender, status)</li>
<li>Process: Query students table with JOIN on fees and payment_deadlines</li>
<li>Output: Student list with fee summary and due dates</li>
<li>Data Store: MySQL students, fees, payment_deadlines tables</li>
</ul>

<p><b>Process 2: Payment Monitoring</b></p>
<ul>
<li>Input: Filter selection (all, today, pending, completed)</li>
<li>Process: Query online_payments table with JOIN on students</li>
<li>Output: Payment transaction list with statistics</li>
<li>Data Store: MySQL online_payments, students tables</li>
</ul>

<p><b>Process 3: Analytics Generation</b></p>
<ul>
<li>Input: Automated (no user input required)</li>
<li>Process: Multiple aggregate SQL queries on fees table with GROUP BY clauses</li>
<li>Output: Collection breakdown, payment method distribution, outstanding student list, KPI metrics</li>
<li>Data Store: MySQL fees, students tables</li>
</ul>

<p><b>Process 4: Send Reminders</b></p>
<ul>
<li>Input: Overdue period selection (0, 7, 14, 30 days)</li>
<li>Process: UPDATE payment_deadlines with reminder_sent flag and incremented reminder_count</li>
<li>Output: Success message with affected student count</li>
<li>Data Store: MySQL payment_deadlines, fees tables</li>
</ul>

<h3>4.3.3 Level 1 Data Flow Diagram – Student Functions</h3>
<p><b>Process 5: Payment Processing</b></p>
<ul>
<li>Input: Student ID, fee selection, payment method, payment details (card/mobile/bank)</li>
<li>Process: Insert record into online_payments table, update fees table status</li>
<li>Output: Redirect to payment receipt with confirmation</li>
<li>Data Store: MySQL online_payments, fees tables</li>
</ul>

<p><b>Process 6: Appointment Booking</b></p>
<ul>
<li>Input: Date, time slot, purpose, notes</li>
<li>Process: Check slot availability (max 5 per slot), insert into appointments table</li>
<li>Output: Success/error message with confirmation</li>
<li>Data Store: MySQL appointments table</li>
</ul>

<p><b>Process 7: Queue Monitoring</b></p>
<ul>
<li>Input: Page refresh (no user input)</li>
<li>Process: Query queue table for serving and waiting tokens</li>
<li>Output: Current serving number, waiting count, estimated wait time, queue list</li>
<li>Data Store: MySQL queue table</li>
</ul>

<h2>4.4 Technology Stack Justification</h2>

<table>
<tr><th>Technology</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Justification</th></tr>
<tr><td>PHP</td><td>Server-side scripting</td><td>Widely supported, extensive documentation, built-in MySQL support, low cost hosting, large developer community. Ideal for educational institution budget constraints.</td></tr>
<tr><td>MySQL</td><td>Relational database</td><td>ACID compliant, free and open-source, excellent performance for structured data, well-integrated with PHP via mysqli extension, supports complex queries with JOINs and aggregations.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Firebase Firestore</td><td>Real-time NoSQL database</td><td>Free tier available (1GB storage, 10GB/month download), real-time listener capability for live notifications, automatic scaling, cloud-hosted with built-in security rules.</td></tr>
<tr><td>HTML5/CSS3</td><td>Frontend markup and styling</td><td>Standard web technologies supported by all modern browsers, responsive design capabilities with CSS Grid and Flexbox, no additional framework dependencies.</td></tr>
<tr><td>JavaScript</td><td>Client-side interactivity</td><td>Universal browser support, vanilla JS used without framework overhead, direct integration with Firebase SDK, DOM manipulation for dynamic UI updates.</td></tr>
<tr><td>XAMPP</td><td>Local development environment</td><td>Bundled Apache, MySQL, PHP, and phpMyAdmin in a single package, cross-platform support, widely used in academic settings, simple configuration.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>4.5 Equipment Configuration</h2>

<h3>4.5.1 Server Requirements</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Component</th><th>Development Environment</th><th>Recommended Production</th></tr>
<tr><td>Operating System</td><td>Windows 10/11</td><td>Ubuntu Server 22.04 LTS or Windows Server 2022</td></tr>
<tr><td>Web Server</td><td>Apache 2.4 (via XAMPP)</td><td>Apache 2.4 or Nginx</td></tr>
<tr><td>PHP Version</td><td>PHP 8.x</td><td>PHP 8.1+</td></tr>
<tr><td>Database</td><td>MySQL 8.x (via XAMPP)</td><td>MySQL 8.x or MariaDB 10.x</td></tr>
<tr><td>RAM</td><td>8 GB</td><td>16 GB minimum, 32 GB recommended</td></tr>
<tr><td>Storage</td><td>500 GB HDD</td><td>256 GB SSD + cloud backup</td></tr>
<tr><td>Processor</td><td>Intel Core i5</td><td>Intel Xeon or equivalent, 4+ cores</td></tr>
<tr><td>Network</td><td>Localhost</td><td>100 Mbps dedicated internet connection</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>4.5.2 Client Requirements</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Component</th><th>Minimum</th><th>Recommended</th></tr>
<tr><td>Web Browser</td><td>Chrome 90+, Firefox 90+, Edge 90+</td><td>Latest Chrome or Firefox</td></tr>
<tr><td>Screen Resolution</td><td>1024x768 pixels</td><td>1920x1080 pixels</td></tr>
<tr><td>Internet Connection</td><td>1 Mbps</td><td>5 Mbps or higher</td></tr>
<tr><td>RAM</td><td>2 GB</td><td>4 GB or higher</td></tr>
<tr><td>JavaScript</td><td>Enabled</td><td>Enabled</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>4.6 Network Architecture</h2>
<p>The FeeFlow system operates on a client-server network architecture. In the development environment, the web server and database server run on the same machine (localhost), and clients access the system through a web browser. For production deployment, a three-tier network architecture is recommended:</p>
<ol>
<li><b>Web Server Tier:</b> Apache or Nginx hosting PHP application files, accessible via HTTPS on port 443.</li>
<li><b>Application Server Tier:</b> PHP processing engine handling business logic and database interactions.</li>
<li><b>Database Server Tier:</b> MySQL server on a separate secured network segment, with access restricted to the application server.</li>
</ol>
<p>Firebase Firestore is accessed directly from the client browser via HTTPS, providing real-time data synchronization without additional server infrastructure. The Firebase Admin SDK can be used for server-to-server communication in production.</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!-- CHAPTER 5: SYSTEM DATA STRUCTURE SPECIFICATIONS -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="page-break"></div>
<h1>CHAPTER 5: SYSTEM DATA STRUCTURE SPECIFICATIONS</h1>

<h2>5.1 Introduction</h2>
<p>This chapter presents the data structure specifications for the FeeFlow system, including logical and physical database designs, entity-relationship diagrams, and detailed schema definitions for all database tables.</p>

<h2>5.2 Logical Database Design</h2>
<p>The FeeFlow database consists of the following entities and their relationships:</p>

<h3>5.2.1 Entity Descriptions</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Entity</th><th>Description</th><th>Key Attributes</th></tr>
<tr><td>Students</td><td>Stores student personal information and academic details</td><td>student_id (PK), name, email, password, gender, class_name, student_status, academic_year, duration, course</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fees</td><td>Records all fee items assigned to students with payment status</td><td>id (PK), student_id (FK), amount, fee_type, status, payment_method, payment_date, created_at</td></tr>
<tr><td>Staff</td><td>Admin user accounts for system access</td><td>id (PK), username, email, password, name, role, created_at</td></tr>
<tr><td>Appointments</td><td>Scheduled appointments between students and finance office</td><td>id (PK), student_id (FK), appointment_date, appointment_time, purpose, notes, status, created_at</td></tr>
<tr><td>Queue</td><td>Digital queue management tracking student visits</td><td>id (PK), token_number, student_id, status, counter_number, created_at</td></tr>
<tr><td>Payment Deadlines</td><td>Due date tracking for pending fee items</td><td>id (PK), student_id (FK), fee_id (FK), due_date, reminder_sent, reminder_count, last_reminder_date, created_at</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>5.3 Physical Database Design</h2>
<p>The physical database schema is implemented in MySQL with the following table structures:</p>

<h3>5.3.1 students Table</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Column</th><th>Data Type</th><th>Constraints</th><th>Description</th></tr>
<tr><td>student_id</td><td>VARCHAR(20)</td><td>PRIMARY KEY</td><td>Unique student identifier (e.g., S101)</td></tr>
<tr><td>name</td><td>VARCHAR(100)</td><td>NOT NULL</td><td>Student full name</td></tr>
<tr><td>email</td><td>VARCHAR(100)</td><td>UNIQUE, NOT NULL</td><td>Student email address</td></tr>
<tr><td>password</td><td>VARCHAR(255)</td><td>NOT NULL</td><td>bcrypt hashed password</td></tr>
<tr><td>gender</td><td>VARCHAR(10)</td><td>NOT NULL, DEFAULT 'Male'</td><td>Student gender</td></tr>
<tr><td>class_name</td><td>VARCHAR(50)</td><td>NOT NULL, DEFAULT 'Primary 1'</td><td>Current class/grade level</td></tr>
<tr><td>student_status</td><td>VARCHAR(20)</td><td>NOT NULL, DEFAULT 'Active'</td><td>Enrollment status (Active/Inactive/Suspended)</td></tr>
<tr><td>academic_year</td><td>VARCHAR(20)</td><td>NOT NULL, DEFAULT '2025/2026'</td><td>Current academic year</td></tr>
<tr><td>duration</td><td>VARCHAR(50)</td><td>NOT NULL, DEFAULT '3 Terms'</td><td>Program duration</td></tr>
<tr><td>course</td><td>VARCHAR(100)</td><td>DEFAULT NULL</td><td>Course/program name (e.g., Lower Primary)</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>5.3.2 fees Table</h3>
